IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `eia-core`

-- Employment Insurance Regulations (SOR/96-332), Part IV: Pilot Project No. 24 (regs 77.995 to 77.999).
-- Piece er5 of the encoding of the Employment Insurance Act and Regulations. LAYER 1: the law, objectively.
-- Text: the plain-text rendering of the Justice Laws consolidation of the Regulations, current to 2026-09-30.
-- It is an unofficial reproduction, and nothing here claims to be verified against the official text.
-- Every `src:N` below is a line of SOR-96-332.txt, the Regulations' raw file, not of the Act's.
--
-- Conventions (from the keel, KEEL-NOTES.md): money is a NUMBER of dollars; a rate is a NUMBER fraction (6.1% is 0.061);
-- a week is the DATE of its Sunday; a date is built with YMD from eia-core; every rule has its own GIVEN.
-- A fact decided by a provision outside this piece's range is a plain input, with a comment
-- "-- out of range: ...". The facts a person states are inputs too; the Commission's judgments are BOOLEAN inputs.
--
-- All the dates in regs 77.996 to 77.999 are dates the text itself states. The text never says
-- on which day reg 77.999 "comes into force"; that day is an input (see NOTES.md, "What the text does not answer").

§ `Regulations Part IV - Pilot Project Establishing Measures to Respond to Major Changes in Economic Conditions`

§§ `Reg 77.995 - Purpose`

-- src:1614 | 77.995 Pilot Project No. 24 is established for the purpose of testing the outcomes of applying certain employment insurance measures to respond to the impacts on employment of major changes in economic conditions.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 77.995 (src:1614)
`reg 77.995 the purpose of Pilot Project No. 24` MEANS
    "testing the outcomes of applying certain employment insurance measures to respond to the impacts on employment of major changes in economic conditions"

§§ `Reg 77.996 and 77.997 - the period of the waiting-period and earnings measures`

-- The period "beginning on March 30, 2025 and ending on October 10, 2026" is stated in both regs 77.996 and 77.997 (a Sunday to a Saturday).
-- src:1617 | 77.996 The Commission may waive the waiting period in respect of any benefit period that begins during the period beginning on March 30, 2025 and ending on October 10, 2026.
-- src:1620 | 77.997 The earnings paid or payable to a claimant by reason of a lay-off or separation from an employment do not constitute earnings for the purposes referred to in subsection 35(2) if
-- src:1621 | (a) the claimant’s benefit period begins during the period beginning on March 30, 2025 and ending on October 10, 2026; or
-- src:1622 | (b) those earnings would, but for this section, be allocated under subsections 36(9) to (11) to a number of weeks the first week of which falls within the period beginning on March 30, 2025 and ending on October 10, 2026.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 77.996, reg 77.997 (src:1617) (src:1620-1622)
`reg 77.996 and reg 77.997 the period from March 30, 2025 to October 10, 2026` MEANS
    `Period of days` OF (YearMonthDay 2025 3 30), (YearMonthDay 2026 10 10)

§§ `Reg 77.996 - Waiting Period`

-- The Commission "may waive" the waiting period of a benefit period that begins in the period: a discretion of the Commission
-- (the person's liability to have it waived or not), so the power and its exercise are two rules.
-- src:1617 | 77.996 The Commission may waive the waiting period in respect of any benefit period that begins during the period beginning on March 30, 2025 and ending on October 10, 2026.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 77.996 (src:1617)
GIVEN `the day on which the benefit period begins` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `reg 77.996 the Commission may waive the waiting period` IF
    `the day` `the day on which the benefit period begins` `falls within` `reg 77.996 and reg 77.997 the period from March 30, 2025 to October 10, 2026`

-- The waiver is the Commission's choice; whether it has chosen to waive is the fact the person is told.
-- src:1617 | 77.996 The Commission may waive the waiting period in respect of any benefit period that begins during the period beginning on March 30, 2025 and ending on October 10, 2026.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 77.996 (src:1617)
GIVEN `the day on which the benefit period begins` IS A DATE
      `the Commission has decided to waive the waiting period` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `reg 77.996 the waiting period is waived` IF
        `reg 77.996 the Commission may waive the waiting period` `the day on which the benefit period begins`
    AND `the Commission has decided to waive the waiting period`

§§ `Reg 77.997 - Earnings for Benefit Purposes`

-- (a) the claimant's benefit period begins in the period; or (b) those earnings would, but for this section, be allocated under
-- reg 36(9) to (11) to weeks the first of which falls within the period. A week falls within the period when all seven of its days do.
-- src:1620 | 77.997 The earnings paid or payable to a claimant by reason of a lay-off or separation from an employment do not constitute earnings for the purposes referred to in subsection 35(2) if
-- src:1621 | (a) the claimant’s benefit period begins during the period beginning on March 30, 2025 and ending on October 10, 2026; or
-- src:1622 | (b) those earnings would, but for this section, be allocated under subsections 36(9) to (11) to a number of weeks the first week of which falls within the period beginning on March 30, 2025 and ending on October 10, 2026.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 77.997 (src:1620-1622)
GIVEN `the earnings are paid or payable to the claimant by reason of a lay-off or separation from an employment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day on which the claimant's benefit period begins` IS A DATE
      `the Sunday of the first week to which those earnings would otherwise be allocated` IS A DATE -- out of range: reg 36(9) to (11) (piece er3)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `reg 77.997 earnings paid by reason of a lay-off or separation do not constitute earnings for the purposes of subsection 35(2)` IF
        `the earnings are paid or payable to the claimant by reason of a lay-off or separation from an employment`
    AND (   `the day` `the day on which the claimant's benefit period begins` `falls within` `reg 77.996 and reg 77.997 the period from March 30, 2025 to October 10, 2026`
         OR (    `the Sunday of the first week to which those earnings would otherwise be allocated`
                     AT LEAST (`reg 77.996 and reg 77.997 the period from March 30, 2025 to October 10, 2026`'s `first day of the period`)
             AND (`the Sunday of the first week to which those earnings would otherwise be allocated` PLUS 6)
                     AT MOST (`reg 77.996 and reg 77.997 the period from March 30, 2025 to October 10, 2026`'s `last day of the period`)))

§§ `Reg 77.998 - Rate of Unemployment`

-- The window is the one the text states for the start of "the later of the weeks referred to in subsection 10(1) of the Act".
-- src:1625 | 77.998 If the later of the weeks referred to in subsection 10(1) of the Act begins during the period beginning on April 6, 2025 and ending on October 11, 2025 and the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant as determined under section 17 of these Regulations is less than 13.1%, the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant is deemed to be
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 77.998 (src:1625)
`reg 77.998 the period in which the later of the weeks referred to in subsection 10(1) of the Act must begin` MEANS
    `Period of days` OF (YearMonthDay 2025 4 6), (YearMonthDay 2025 10 11)

-- The rate that applies is deemed to be 7.1% (rate 6.1% or less), the rate plus 1% (more than 6.1% and less than 12.1%) or 13.1% (12.1% or more),
-- but only when the later week begins in the window and the reg 17 rate is less than 13.1%. Otherwise nothing is deemed: the reg 17 rate stays.
-- src:1625 | 77.998 If the later of the weeks referred to in subsection 10(1) of the Act begins during the period beginning on April 6, 2025 and ending on October 11, 2025 and the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant as determined under section 17 of these Regulations is less than 13.1%, the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant is deemed to be
-- src:1626 | (a) 7.1%, if the rate determined under section 17 is 6.1% or less;
-- src:1627 | (b) the sum of 1% and the rate determined under section 17, if the latter is greater than 6.1% and less than 12.1%; or
-- src:1628 | (c) 13.1%, if the rate determined under section 17 is 12.1% or greater.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 77.998 (src:1625-1628)
GIVEN `the Sunday on which the later of the weeks referred to in subsection 10(1) of the Act begins` IS A DATE -- out of range: s 10(1) of the Act (piece ei1)
      `the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant as determined under section 17` IS A NUMBER -- out of range: reg 17 (piece er1)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `reg 77.998 the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant` IS
    IF     `the day` `the Sunday on which the later of the weeks referred to in subsection 10(1) of the Act begins` `falls within` `reg 77.998 the period in which the later of the weeks referred to in subsection 10(1) of the Act must begin`
       AND `the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant as determined under section 17` LESS THAN 13.1%
    THEN BRANCH
             IF `the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant as determined under section 17` AT MOST 6.1%
             THEN 7.1%
             IF `the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant as determined under section 17` LESS THAN 12.1%
             THEN `the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant as determined under section 17` PLUS 1%
             OTHERWISE 13.1%
    ELSE `the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant as determined under section 17`

§§ `Reg 77.999(1) - long-tenured worker`

-- "long-tenured worker": paid less than 36 weeks of regular benefits in the 156 weeks before the benefit period, and who,
-- according to assessed tax returns, paid at least 30% of the maximum annual employee's premium in 7 of the 10 years before the
-- benefit period or, if the return for the year before the benefit period is not yet filed or assessed, in 7 of the 10 years before that year.
-- src:1631 | 77.999 (1) In this section, long-tenured worker means a claimant who was paid less than 36 weeks of regular benefits in the 156 weeks before the beginning of their benefit period and who, according to their income tax returns for which notices of assessment have been sent by the Canada Revenue Agency, paid at least 30% of the maximum annual employee’s premium in 7 of the 10 years before the beginning of their benefit period or, if their income tax return for the year before the beginning of their benefit period has not yet been filed with that Agency or a notice of assessment for that year has not yet been sent by that Agency, in 7 of the 10 years before that year.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 77.999(1) (src:1631)
GIVEN `weeks of regular benefits paid in the 156 weeks before the beginning of the benefit period` IS A NUMBER
      `the return for the year before the beginning of the benefit period has been filed and a notice of assessment for that year sent` IS A BOOLEAN
      `years among the 10 years before the beginning of the benefit period in which at least 30% of the maximum annual employee's premium was paid` IS A NUMBER
      `years among the 10 years before the year before the beginning of the benefit period in which at least 30% of the maximum annual employee's premium was paid` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `reg 77.999(1) long-tenured worker` IF
        `weeks of regular benefits paid in the 156 weeks before the beginning of the benefit period` LESS THAN 36
    AND (   IF `the return for the year before the beginning of the benefit period has been filed and a notice of assessment for that year sent`
            THEN `years among the 10 years before the beginning of the benefit period in which at least 30% of the maximum annual employee's premium was paid` AT LEAST 7
            ELSE `years among the 10 years before the year before the beginning of the benefit period in which at least 30% of the maximum annual employee's premium was paid` AT LEAST 7)

§§ `Reg 77.999(2) and (3) - Benefit period extended by 20 weeks`

-- src:1632 | (2) A benefit period that is established in accordance with section 9 of the Act, that begins during the period beginning on June 15, 2025 and ending on October 10, 2026 and that has not ended in accordance with paragraph 10(8)(d) of the Act before the day on which this section comes into force is extended by 20 weeks if the claimant is a long-tenured worker and has been paid at least one week of regular benefits or benefits by virtue of section 25 of the Act in that benefit period.
-- src:1633 | (3) In respect of any benefit period that begins during the period beginning on June 15, 2025 and ending on October 10, 2026, subsection 10(14) of the Act is adapted as follows:
-- src:1634 | (14)An extension under one or more of subsections (10) to (13.02) and subsection 77.999(2) of the Employment Insurance Regulations must not result in a benefit period of more than 104 weeks.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 77.999(2), reg 77.999(3) (src:1632-1634)
`reg 77.999(2) and (3) the period in which a benefit period must begin` MEANS
    `Period of days` OF (YearMonthDay 2025 6 15), (YearMonthDay 2026 10 10)

-- The benefit period (established under s 9 of the Act) must begin in the period and must not have ended under paragraph 10(8)(d)
-- of the Act before the day the section comes into force; the claimant must be a long-tenured worker who has been paid at least one week
-- of regular benefits, or of benefits by virtue of s 25, in that benefit period. The extension is then 20 weeks; otherwise this section extends nothing.
-- src:1632 | (2) A benefit period that is established in accordance with section 9 of the Act, that begins during the period beginning on June 15, 2025 and ending on October 10, 2026 and that has not ended in accordance with paragraph 10(8)(d) of the Act before the day on which this section comes into force is extended by 20 weeks if the claimant is a long-tenured worker and has been paid at least one week of regular benefits or benefits by virtue of section 25 of the Act in that benefit period.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 77.999(2) (src:1632)
GIVEN `the benefit period was established in accordance with section 9 of the Act` IS A BOOLEAN -- out of range: s 9 of the Act (piece ei1)
      `the day on which the benefit period begins` IS A DATE
      `the day on which the benefit period ended in accordance with paragraph 10(8)(d) of the Act, if it did` IS A MAYBE DATE -- out of range: s 10(8)(d) of the Act (piece ei1)
      `the day on which this section comes into force` IS A DATE -- the text does not state it
      `the claimant is a long-tenured worker` IS A BOOLEAN
      `at least one week of regular benefits or of benefits by virtue of section 25 of the Act has been paid in the benefit period` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `reg 77.999(2) the number of weeks by which the benefit period is extended` IS
    IF     `the benefit period was established in accordance with section 9 of the Act`
       AND `the day` `the day on which the benefit period begins` `falls within` `reg 77.999(2) and (3) the period in which a benefit period must begin`
       AND (    CONSIDER `the day on which the benefit period ended in accordance with paragraph 10(8)(d) of the Act, if it did`
                WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE
                WHEN JUST ended THEN ended AT LEAST `the day on which this section comes into force`)
       AND `the claimant is a long-tenured worker`
       AND `at least one week of regular benefits or of benefits by virtue of section 25 of the Act has been paid in the benefit period`
    THEN 20
    ELSE 0

-- (3) For a benefit period beginning in the period, subsection 10(14) of the Act reads as the text sets out: an extension under
-- one or more of subsections 10(10) to (13.02) and reg 77.999(2) must not result in a benefit period of more than 104 weeks.
-- Outside the period the Act's own subsection 10(14) governs (piece ei1) and this rule answers NOTHING.
-- src:1633 | (3) In respect of any benefit period that begins during the period beginning on June 15, 2025 and ending on October 10, 2026, subsection 10(14) of the Act is adapted as follows:
-- src:1634 | (14)An extension under one or more of subsections (10) to (13.02) and subsection 77.999(2) of the Employment Insurance Regulations must not result in a benefit period of more than 104 weeks.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 77.999(3) (src:1633-1634)
GIVEN `the day on which the benefit period begins` IS A DATE
      `the length in weeks that the benefit period would have after the extensions under subsections 10(10) to (13.02) of the Act and subsection 77.999(2)` IS A NUMBER -- out of range: s 10(10) to (13.02) of the Act (piece ei1)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `reg 77.999(3) the length in weeks of the benefit period after its extensions, held to 104 weeks` IS
    IF `the day` `the day on which the benefit period begins` `falls within` `reg 77.999(2) and (3) the period in which a benefit period must begin`
    THEN JUST (min 104 `the length in weeks that the benefit period would have after the extensions under subsections 10(10) to (13.02) of the Act and subsection 77.999(2)`)
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Reg 77.999(4) - Maximum weeks of benefits when the benefit period has been extended`

-- (a) the maximum number of weeks determined under s 12(2) or (2.3) of the Act, for a reason other than one in s 12(3), is increased by 20 weeks.
-- src:1635 | (4) If a claimant’s benefit period has been extended under subsection (2),
-- src:1636 | (a) the maximum number of weeks determined under subsection 12(2) or (2.3) of the Act for which benefits may be paid to the claimant in that benefit period because of a reason other than one mentioned in subsection 12(3) of the Act is increased by 20 weeks; and
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 77.999(4)(a) (src:1635-1636)
GIVEN `the maximum number of weeks determined under subsection 12(2) or (2.3) of the Act` IS A NUMBER -- out of range: s 12(2), (2.3) of the Act (piece ei1)
      `the benefit period has been extended under subsection 77.999(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `reg 77.999(4)(a) the maximum number of weeks of benefits for a reason other than one mentioned in subsection 12(3) of the Act` IS
    IF `the benefit period has been extended under subsection 77.999(2)`
    THEN `the maximum number of weeks determined under subsection 12(2) or (2.3) of the Act` PLUS 20
    ELSE `the maximum number of weeks determined under subsection 12(2) or (2.3) of the Act`

-- (b) the reference in s 12(6) of the Act to 50 weeks is read as 70 weeks. The text names 50 as the figure of s 12(6) that it changes.
-- src:1635 | (4) If a claimant’s benefit period has been extended under subsection (2),
-- src:1637 | (b) the reference in subsection 12(6) of the Act to 50 weeks is to be read as a reference to 70 weeks.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 77.999(4)(b) (src:1635) (src:1637)
GIVEN `the benefit period has been extended under subsection 77.999(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `reg 77.999(4)(b) the number of weeks that subsection 12(6) of the Act refers to` IS
    IF `the benefit period has been extended under subsection 77.999(2)`
    THEN 70
    ELSE 50

§§ `Reg 77.999(5) - The additional weeks stay payable`

-- Additional weeks payable under this section do not cease to be payable by reason only that the claimant ceases to be a long-tenured worker
-- during the benefit period. So the cessation is accepted as an input and does not change the answer; only some other ground can end the weeks.
-- src:1638 | (5) Additional weeks of benefits that are payable under this section do not cease to be payable by reason only that the claimant ceases to be a long-tenured worker during the benefit period.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 77.999(5) (src:1638)
GIVEN `the benefit period has been extended under subsection 77.999(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the claimant has ceased to be a long-tenured worker during the benefit period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `some reason other than that cessation ends the right to the additional weeks` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `reg 77.999(5) the additional weeks are still payable` IF
        `the benefit period has been extended under subsection 77.999(2)`
    AND (NOT `some reason other than that cessation ends the right to the additional weeks`)
